# .NET Framework → .NET 8 Migration: BackgroundProcessor

## Critical Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 8 — it's completely removed
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also throws** — the entire abort mechanism is gone
3. **`ThreadAbortException` is never raised** by the runtime

## Why It Was Removed

`Thread.Abort()` was inherently unsafe — it could interrupt code mid-execution (even inside `finally` blocks or static constructors), corrupt shared state, and cause orphaned locks. .NET 8 replaces this with **cooperative cancellation** via `CancellationToken`.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls can be interrupted cooperatively
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cooperative cancellation requested — clean up and exit
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        timeout ??= TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);

        // Signal cancellation (cooperative — no thread is forcibly killed)
        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait for graceful shutdown, but don't wait forever
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(timeout.Value)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Worker didn't finish in time — log a warning.
            // There is NO forced kill equivalent in .NET 8 by design.
            Log.Warning("Worker did not stop within timeout");
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _workerTask?.GetAwaiter().GetResult(); // block if disposing synchronously
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## If You Can't Make It Fully Async

When `GetNextItem()` is a **synchronous blocking call** you don't control (e.g., legacy library), you can still use cooperative cancellation:

```csharp
private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
{
    while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        try
        {
            var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token where possible
            token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // check between steps
            ProcessItem(item);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            RollbackCurrentTransaction();
            break;
        }
        finally
        {
            FlushBuffers();
        }
    }
}
```

## Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **`Thread.Abort` compiles but throws at runtime** | It exists in the API surface but throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| **`catch (ThreadAbortException)` is dead code** | The runtime never throws it — your rollback logic silently disappears |
| **Blocking calls ignore cancellation** | If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a socket/queue with no token support, cancellation won't interrupt it — consider wrapping with a timeout |
| **`Thread.Sleep` → `Task.Delay`** | `Thread.Sleep` blocks a thread pool thread; `Task.Delay` is non-blocking and accepts a `CancellationToken` |
| **`_running` volatile bool is fragile** | `CancellationToken` is the idiomatic replacement — it integrates with async APIs, `WaitHandle`, and `linked` token sources |
| **Dispose the `CancellationTokenSource`** | It holds a `WaitHandle` internally; leaking it can cause resource exhaustion |

## Migration Checklist

1. Replace `volatile bool` → `CancellationTokenSource` + `CancellationToken`
2. Replace `new Thread(...)` → `Task.Run(...)`
3. Delete `Thread.Abort()` / `ResetAbort()` — replace with `_cts.Cancel()`
4. Replace `catch (ThreadAbortException)` → `catch (OperationCanceledException)`
5. Pass `CancellationToken` through to all blocking/async calls
6. Implement `IDisposable` to clean up the `CancellationTokenSource`
7. Replace synchronous `Stop()` with `async StopAsync()` where possible
